{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "140",
    "case_summary": "A 60-year-old woman presented with a 3-month history of epigastric abdominal pain, unintentional 33 lb weight loss, and intermittent melena. Her medical history was notable for irritable bowel syndrome and diet-controlled gluten intolerance; she denied tobacco and alcohol use. On examination, her abdomen was soft and nontender without organomegaly. Laboratory testing showed normocytic anemia with a hemoglobin level of 11.6 g/dL. An abdominal CT scan was unremarkable. Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy revealed a 1.5 cm semipedunculated polyp (Paris Isp) in the gastric body, the same color as the surrounding mucosa, with a smooth surface lacking overlying mucus or prominent vessels. There was a heterogeneous surface pattern with uniform pitting near the base; no ulcerations or depressions were noted. The polyp was completely resected by snare polypectomy, and several other small sessile polyps were biopsied.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Gastric adenomyoma\nB. Well\nC. Gastritis cystica polyposa/profunda\nD. Gastric duplication cyst\nE. Gastric diverticulum\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
